The board is oriented with row 0 at the top. The current pieces yield the following column heights (number of occupied cells):  
[1, 2, 5, 4, 2, 0, 0].  

Player x has no placement that completes four-in-a-row:  
- column 0 \(\to\) (4,0) yields at most three consecutive x’s;  
- column 1 \(\to\) (3,1) yields at most three consecutive x’s (vertical or diagonal);  
- column 2 \(\to\) (0,2) yields only isolated pieces;  
- column 3 \(\to\) (1,3) yields only isolated pieces;  
- column 4 \(\to\) (3,4) yields at most two consecutive x’s;  
- column 5 \(\to\) (5,5) yields a diagonal of exactly three x’s (blocked by an o);  
- column 6 \(\to\) (5,6) yields only isolated pieces.  

Player o, however, possesses an immediate winning threat. The three o’s already stacked in column 2 occupy rows 1-3; placing an o at (0,2) produces a vertical four-in-a-row. Exhaustive examination of every other legal cell for o shows that none of them completes four-in-a-row.  

Consequently any move other than column 2 allows o to win on the subsequent turn. Placing x at (0,2) occupies the critical cell, leaves o with no immediate win, and is therefore the unique optimal action.

Final Answer: 2

